# Stage 8 — the form theme slug, and the block name.
#
# Both were held back from stage 7 because they are stored values rather than
# class names, so each needs its rename paired with a read-side migration.
#
# gravity-theme is the form theme slug. It is saved per form in the form meta,
# saved site-wide in the rg_gforms_default_theme option, and validated against
# array( 'legacy', 'gravity-theme', 'orbital' ) — so renaming it alone would
# make every existing form fail that check and silently fall back to the
# default. The migration goes in KDNAForms::normalize_theme_slug(), which both
# get_default_theme() and get_form_theme_slug() run their value through.
#
# gravityforms/form is the Gutenberg block name. This one is not a migration at
# all, it is a half-applied rename that currently has the block broken in both
# directions: PHP registers kdnaforms/form and keys its editor config under
# that name, while the JS bundles call registerBlockType("gravityforms/form")
# and read block_editor["gravityforms/form"]. A block inserted from the editor
# therefore saves wp:gravityforms/form, which PHP has no renderer for and which
# renders as nothing, while the embed endpoint writes wp:kdnaforms/form, which
# the editor does not recognise. Moving the JS side onto the name PHP already
# uses makes the two agree. Nothing needs preserving for old content, because
# a stored wp:gravityforms/form block has never had a renderer to begin with.

gravity-theme                                              kdna-theme
gravityforms/form                                          kdnaforms/form
